<<<UNTRUSTED:Tool="semantic_version":...>The issue lies in the implementation of the '~' operator in the semver crate. Currently, it's interpreting '~' as '~<patch>', which means it's selecting the latest patch version available, not the highest version below the major/minor specified.

To fix this, we need to change the '~' operator to interpret its operand as a range like '>=<patch> <!=<major>.<minor>.0>'.

Here's a possible patch for the relevant part of the semver crate in Rust:

```rust
// Old (erroneous) implementation
fn version_from_range(range: &str) -> Result<Version, Error> {
    // ... (irrelevant parts of the function)
    let (low, high) = range.split('<').collect_tuple().unwrap();
    let low = Version::parse(low)?;
    let high = Version::parse(high)?;
    Ok(VersionRng::new(low, high.into()))
}

// New (corrected) implementation
fn version_from_range(range: &str) -> Result<Version, Error> {
    // ... (irrelevant parts of the function)
    if range.starts_with("~") {
        let mut parts = range[1..].splitn(2..2, '.');
        let low = Version::parse(parts.next().unwrap())?;
        if let Some(high) = parts.next() {
            let high = Version::parse(high)?;
            Ok(VersionRng::new(low.with_patch(0), high.into()))
                // Adjust to capture greater than low patch and less than high minor
        } else {
            Err(Error::new(ErrorKind::InvalidRange, "Invalid version range ~"))
        }
    } else {
        // Handle other ranges as before (not changed)
    }
}
```

This corrected version makes sure that '~1.2.3' would match versions from '1.2.3' up to '1.2.x<1.3.0', as intended. Remember to update test cases accordingly to cover this new behavior.

